  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder air will be accompanied by scattered wet\nsnow and rain showers through the weekend with minor snow \naccumulations expected across the higher terrain.\n\nA sufficiently-cold/moist northwesterly to northerly mean low-level\nflow will continue to produce some upslope flow-induced and \nlake-effect precip tonight south of the Lower Great Lakes. The \nshort fetch across the lakes, shallow nature of the background \nsynoptic moisture, and relatively low inversion heights should \nhelp to keep this precip strictly of the light/nuisance variety.\nThe marginally cold air mass will result in primarily wet snow \nacross the higher terrain and at lower elevations, but rain may\nmix-in at times, especially at lower elevations. Lows are \nexpected to reach the mid 20's to mid 30's around daybreak.\n\nOn Saturday through Saturday night, primarily light lake-effect\nand upslope flow-induced precipitation will continue generally \nsouth of the Lower Great Lakes as the northwesterly to northerly \nmean low-level flow remains sufficiently-cold/moist. However, \nprecip should undergo an uptick in coverage and become somewhat \nsteadier late this morning through early evening as a mid/upper\nlevel trough axis and accompanying area of greater and deeper \nsynoptic moisture in the low/mid-levels advance southeastward \nacross our region, and moist isentropic ascent precedes the \ntrough axis aloft, as does the seeder-feeder process over and \ndownwind of the Lower Great Lakes. With vertical profiles of \ntemperature and wet-bulb temperature remaining marginal for rain\nversus snow (and actually warming a little compared to Friday \ndue to some weak WAA at the surface and aloft ahead of the \ntrough axis), the lower elevations should see mainly wet snow to\nstart the day before precip changes to mainly rain for the late\nmorning through afternoon and then changes to a rain/wet snow \nmix this evening and especially overnight tonight. However, the \nhigher terrain should experience primarily wet snow, perhaps \nmixed with rain at times, especially late this morning through \nearly evening. Daytime highs should reach the mid 30's to lower \n40's today and be followed by overnight lows in the mid 20's to \nmid 30's around daybreak Sunday. \n\nRidging at the surface and aloft will build gradually eastward \ninto our region on Sunday through Sunday night. Subsidence \naccompanying the ridge will result in gradual lowering of \ninversion heights and a commensurate drying out of the prime \ndendritic snow growth zone aloft. This will result in lingering \nisolated to scattered upslope flow-induced and lake-effect wet \nsnow and/or rain showers south and southeast of the lakes to end \ngradually from west to east Sunday afternoon through evening. \nDry weather is then expected region-wide through Sunday night. \nAdditional snow accumulations through Sunday are expected to be \n1\" or less in the higher terrain. At lower elevations, any snow \naccumulations are expected to be 0.5\" or less. Highs should \nreach mainly the mid 30's to mid 40's late Sunday afternoon and \nbe followed by overnight lows in the mid 20's to mid 30's around\ndaybreak Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with temperatures \ntrending warmer by midweek.\n\nExpansive/strengthening ridging at the surface and aloft will \ncontinue to build from the Great Plains and across the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley right through the middle of next week. \nThis feature will bring a return to dry weather and should be\naccompanied by mainly clear to partly cloudy sky. A cool air \nmass ahead of the surface high will linger across Western and \nCentral NY on Monday. Temperatures should then rebound nicely \nthrough midweek as the axis of the ridge slides east of our \nlongitude and allows a warmer southerly to southwesterly flow to\ndevelop across our area. It still appears this low-level WAA \nregime will contribute to daytime highs climbing back to above-\nnormal levels by Thursday, when late afternoon highs should \nreach mainly the mid 50's to lower 60's.",
